1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,722
Here is Asha's loop from Chapter 7.

2
00:00:02,742 --> 00:00:08,488
To serve three tables, she needed three
separate lines: a start, a condition, and

3
00:00:08,508 --> 00:00:09,200
an update.

4
00:00:09,233 --> 00:00:11,243
Three places to make a mistake.

5
00:00:11,263 --> 00:00:14,057
Forget the update, and the loop never
ends.

6
00:00:14,653 --> 00:00:16,688
This is Code Quest, Chapter 8.

7
00:00:16,708 --> 00:00:19,563
Today you meet a loop that counts for you.

8
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:25,350
And an AI assistant will rewrite Asha's
loyalty card, promise the same ten stamps,

9
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:27,320
and quietly lose a free tea.

10
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:29,895
So here is today's mission.

11
00:00:29,915 --> 00:00:34,180
A price chart for the cafe counter, from 1
tea to 10 teas.

12
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:39,180
Every 5th tea is free, and the chart works
out the free teas by itself.

13
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,680
Ten rows, from a loop with no counter of
its own.

14
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:43,751
Let's build it.

15
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:47,647
Write a for loop that repeats once for
each number in a range.

16
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:51,107
Use range with a start, a stop and a step.

17
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:53,523
Go through every character of a string.

18
00:00:53,567 --> 00:00:55,827
Add up a total inside a for loop.

19
00:00:55,867 --> 00:00:59,384
And compare two loops, and explain which
one is clearer.

20
00:00:59,983 --> 00:01:05,908
Often you know in advance how many times
something must happen: 5 receipt lines, 10

21
00:01:05,928 --> 00:01:07,484
stamps, 12 characters.

22
00:01:07,504 --> 00:01:10,348
For these jobs, Python has the for loop.

23
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:14,472
A for loop takes values from a sequence,
one at a time.

24
00:01:14,492 --> 00:01:19,880
A sequence is a group of values in a fixed
order, like numbers, or the characters of

25
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:20,460
a string.

26
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:24,183
On each pass, the loop variable gets the
next value.

27
00:01:24,203 --> 00:01:26,533
Here, table is 1, then 2, then 3.

28
00:01:26,553 --> 00:01:29,310
When no values are left, the loop ends.

29
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:31,707
And here is the most important rule.

30
00:01:31,727 --> 00:01:33,968
Range stops before the stop value.

31
00:01:33,988 --> 00:01:36,362
Range with 1 and 4 gives 1, 2 and 3.

32
00:01:36,382 --> 00:01:36,894
Never 4.

33
00:01:37,487 --> 00:01:38,967
Think of a stamp card.

34
00:01:38,987 --> 00:01:43,467
You move from box to box, one at a time,
and you never skip a box.

35
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:48,588
But unlike a stamp card, range leaves out
the number at the end.

36
00:01:48,608 --> 00:01:51,780
Range with 1 and 4 has boxes 1, 2 and 3.

37
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,217
There is no box 4.

38
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:54,601
Quick check.

39
00:01:54,621 --> 00:01:57,469
Which numbers does range with 2 and 5
give?

40
00:02:08,847 --> 00:02:09,007
D.

41
00:02:09,027 --> 00:02:09,996
2, 3 and 4.

42
00:02:10,016 --> 00:02:13,144
It starts at 2, and stops before 5.

43
00:02:13,753 --> 00:02:14,850
Take a breather.

44
00:02:14,870 --> 00:02:16,873
Here is what you know so far.

45
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,567
Next, Asha rewrites her table loop from
Chapter 7.

46
00:02:25,317 --> 00:02:25,850
Step 1.

47
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:29,408
Here is the Chapter 7 loop, written with
for.

48
00:02:29,428 --> 00:02:32,967
Line 2 says: for table in range with 1 and
4.

49
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,178
Line 3 prints the table number.

50
00:02:35,198 --> 00:02:38,510
It is indented, so it is the block of the
loop.

51
00:02:38,533 --> 00:02:40,334
Line 4 is not indented.

52
00:02:40,354 --> 00:02:42,629
It runs once, after the loop.

53
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:56,894
Three times, and the last table is 3.

54
00:02:56,914 --> 00:02:58,560
Range stops before 4.

55
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:01,918
And look: no start line, and no update
line.

56
00:03:01,938 --> 00:03:03,511
Range does both jobs.

57
00:03:03,531 --> 00:03:06,091
One place to get right, not three.

58
00:03:06,683 --> 00:03:07,122
Step 2.

59
00:03:07,142 --> 00:03:08,564
Range has three forms.

60
00:03:08,584 --> 00:03:10,662
With one number, it starts at 0.

61
00:03:10,682 --> 00:03:13,873
So range with 4 gives four values,
starting at 0.

62
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:17,900
With two numbers, you give the start and
the stop.

63
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:22,388
With three numbers, the third is the step:
how much to add each time.

64
00:03:22,408 --> 00:03:24,203
A negative step counts down.

65
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:29,103
End equals a space, from Chapter 1, keeps
each loop's numbers on one line.

66
00:03:29,123 --> 00:03:31,085
The empty print ends the line.

67
00:03:42,447 --> 00:03:45,419
Range with 4 gives 0, 1, 2 and 3.

68
00:03:45,439 --> 00:03:47,867
Four values, starting at 0.

69
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:50,780
Range with 2 and 6 gives 2 to 5.

70
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:54,139
And 10, counting down by 3: 10, 7, 4, 1.

71
00:03:54,159 --> 00:03:59,848
The next value would be minus 2, and that
is past the stop value, 0.

72
00:04:00,450 --> 00:04:00,953
Step 3.

73
00:04:00,973 --> 00:04:05,955
A for loop can also take the characters of
a string, one at a time.

74
00:04:05,975 --> 00:04:08,792
Here is the order code from Chapter 1.

75
00:04:08,812 --> 00:04:11,480
Line 3 starts a hyphen counter at 0.

76
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:13,663
Line 4: for character in code.

77
00:04:13,683 --> 00:04:18,030
On each pass, character is the next
letter, digit or hyphen.

78
00:04:18,067 --> 00:04:20,947
Line 5 prints it, with a space after it.

79
00:04:20,967 --> 00:04:26,457
Lines 6 and 7 add 1 to the counter, but
only when the character is a hyphen.

80
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:30,269
Line 8, an empty print, ends the line of
characters.

81
00:04:30,289 --> 00:04:35,152
Line 9 prints the length, with len from
Chapter 3, and the hyphens.

82
00:04:46,513 --> 00:04:49,893
The block runs 12 times, once for each
character.

83
00:04:49,933 --> 00:04:50,979
And 2 hyphens.

84
00:04:50,999 --> 00:04:53,948
The if adds 1 only on those two passes.

85
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:55,029
Step 4.

86
00:04:55,049 --> 00:04:57,379
Asha adds up three days of sales.

87
00:04:57,399 --> 00:05:00,940
Line 2 sets the total to 0, once, before
the loop.

88
00:05:00,967 --> 00:05:03,790
Line 3: for day in range with 1 and 4.

89
00:05:03,810 --> 00:05:07,307
So there are three passes, for days 1, 2
and 3.

90
00:05:07,333 --> 00:05:09,769
Line 4 asks for the sales of that day.

91
00:05:09,789 --> 00:05:12,937
The f string puts the day number in the
question.

92
00:05:12,957 --> 00:05:15,263
Line 5 adds the answer to the total.

93
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:20,965
Lines 6 and 7 run once, after the loop:
the total, and the average, which is the

94
00:05:20,985 --> 00:05:22,315
total divided by 3.

95
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:37,381
Line 4 asks three times, once per pass.

96
00:05:37,401 --> 00:05:41,770
Lines 6 and 7 print once each, after the
loop.

97
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:49,670
The total is 361.75, and the average is
120.58, with two decimal places.

98
00:05:50,250 --> 00:05:50,855
Step 5.

99
00:05:50,875 --> 00:05:53,267
The kettle needs 5 minutes.

100
00:05:53,287 --> 00:05:59,340
Range with 5, 0 and minus 1 starts at 5,
and takes away 1 each time.

101
00:05:59,367 --> 00:06:01,832
Line 3 prints the minutes left.

102
00:06:01,852 --> 00:06:03,997
Line 4 runs after the loop.

103
00:06:15,347 --> 00:06:15,480
1.

104
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:19,077
The stop value is 0, and range stops
before it.

105
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:21,575
Then line 4: tea is ready.

106
00:06:22,187 --> 00:06:24,123
So which loop should you choose?

107
00:06:24,143 --> 00:06:28,157
Use for when you know the number of passes
before the loop starts.

108
00:06:28,177 --> 00:06:30,847
Five minutes, ten stamps, twelve
characters.

109
00:06:30,867 --> 00:06:35,440
Use while when you wait for something,
like a valid answer, or the word done.

110
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:38,423
Then you cannot know how many passes it
will take.

111
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:39,876
Quick check.

112
00:06:39,896 --> 00:06:44,550
A program asks for a PIN until the
customer types the right one.

113
00:06:44,570 --> 00:06:46,083
Which loop fits best?

114
00:06:57,447 --> 00:06:57,551
C.

115
00:06:57,571 --> 00:06:58,361
A while loop.

116
00:06:58,381 --> 00:07:02,782
You wait for the right answer, so you
cannot know the number of passes.

117
00:07:03,387 --> 00:07:03,995
Breather.

118
00:07:04,015 --> 00:07:06,297
Sit back, and take a slow breath.

119
00:07:06,333 --> 00:07:08,536
You now have every piece of the mission.

120
00:07:13,117 --> 00:07:14,672
Back to the mission.

121
00:07:14,692 --> 00:07:16,878
Line 2 stores the tea price.

122
00:07:16,898 --> 00:07:19,557
Line 3 prints the column headings.

123
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:22,224
Line 4: for teas in range with 1 and 11.

124
00:07:22,244 --> 00:07:23,480
That gives 1 to 10.

125
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:29,320
Line 5 counts the free teas: teas, floor
divided by 5, from Chapter 2.

126
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:31,322
4 teas give 0 free teas.

127
00:07:31,342 --> 00:07:32,490
5 teas give 1.

128
00:07:32,533 --> 00:07:35,811
Line 6 charges only the teas that are not
free.

129
00:07:35,831 --> 00:07:40,371
Line 7 lines up the columns with the
format codes from Chapter 3.

130
00:07:40,391 --> 00:07:42,967
And line 8 runs once, after the loop.

131
00:07:54,347 --> 00:07:55,951
5 teas and 10 teas.

132
00:07:55,971 --> 00:08:00,997
At 5 teas, one tea is free, so 5 cost
14.00, the same as 4.

133
00:08:01,033 --> 00:08:06,883
At 10 teas, two are free, so 10 cost
28.00, the same as 9.

134
00:08:06,933 --> 00:08:08,298
Mission complete!

135
00:08:08,318 --> 00:08:10,171
Next, the loyalty card.

136
00:08:10,191 --> 00:08:12,777
Asha wrote it with a while loop.

137
00:08:12,797 --> 00:08:18,151
Now she will rewrite it with an AI
assistant, one stage at a time.

138
00:08:18,750 --> 00:08:21,764
This chapter's AI skill is comparing
loops.

139
00:08:21,784 --> 00:08:27,196
When an AI writes a loop for a job you
already solved, run both with the same

140
00:08:27,216 --> 00:08:27,690
values.

141
00:08:27,733 --> 00:08:29,939
Compare the outputs line by line.

142
00:08:29,959 --> 00:08:34,457
Then decide which loop is clearer for the
next person who reads it.

143
00:08:34,477 --> 00:08:37,761
And the safety habits from Chapter 1 still
apply.

144
00:08:38,353 --> 00:08:39,267
Stage 1: plan.

145
00:08:39,287 --> 00:08:43,868
Asha's loyalty card has 10 stamps, and
every 5th stamp is a free tea.

146
00:08:43,888 --> 00:08:48,202
She wrote it with a while loop, and wants
to rewrite it with for.

147
00:08:48,222 --> 00:08:51,203
She asks: is for a good fit, and which
range?

148
00:08:51,223 --> 00:08:52,003
No code yet.

149
00:08:52,033 --> 00:08:56,221
The AI says: a for loop, because you know
the number of passes.

150
00:08:56,241 --> 00:09:00,630
And range with 1 and 11, because the stop
value is never included.

151
00:09:00,650 --> 00:09:01,431
A good plan.

152
00:09:02,053 --> 00:09:03,131
Stage 2: write.

153
00:09:03,151 --> 00:09:06,864
Before the rewrite, Asha checks her own
while loop.

154
00:09:06,884 --> 00:09:12,133
She asks the AI to list every value of
stamp, without changing anything.

155
00:09:12,167 --> 00:09:14,529
The AI lists 1 to 10: ten passes.

156
00:09:14,549 --> 00:09:20,017
Stamps 5 and 10 have no remainder when
divided by 5, so they print free tea.

157
00:09:20,037 --> 00:09:22,183
Asha's own list says the same.

158
00:09:22,787 --> 00:09:23,653
She runs it.

159
00:09:23,673 --> 00:09:26,460
Ten stamps, with free tea on 5 and 10.

160
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:32,148
And notice the three lines from Chapter 7
again: a start, a condition, and an

161
00:09:32,168 --> 00:09:32,665
update.

162
00:09:33,287 --> 00:09:34,660
Stage 3: generate.

163
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:41,084
Asha asks for a for version with the same
output: 10 stamps, with free tea on 5 and

164
00:09:41,104 --> 00:09:41,317
10.

165
00:09:41,367 --> 00:09:45,835
The AI gives a short for loop, and
promises the same 10 lines.

166
00:09:45,855 --> 00:09:47,717
It sounds completely sure.

167
00:09:47,767 --> 00:09:50,856
Look at line 2, and remember where range
stops.

168
00:10:02,213 --> 00:10:02,574
Nine.

169
00:10:02,594 --> 00:10:05,315
Range with 1 and 10 stops before 10.

170
00:10:05,335 --> 00:10:07,142
Asha suspects a problem.

171
00:10:07,162 --> 00:10:09,425
Now she proves it with checks.

172
00:10:10,020 --> 00:10:11,015
Stage 4: test.

173
00:10:11,035 --> 00:10:16,741
Asha asks for four checks, each with the
exact expected result: the first line,

174
00:10:16,761 --> 00:10:20,220
stamp 5, the last line, and the number of
lines.

175
00:10:20,267 --> 00:10:21,539
The AI lists them.

176
00:10:21,559 --> 00:10:24,626
The last line should be stamp 10, free
tea.

177
00:10:24,646 --> 00:10:26,709
And there should be 10 lines.

178
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:31,745
She runs the AI's version, and reads the
last line first.

179
00:10:31,765 --> 00:10:33,460
Stamp 9, an empty box.

180
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:35,518
She counts the lines: nine.

181
00:10:35,538 --> 00:10:37,859
The second free tea is missing.

182
00:10:38,453 --> 00:10:40,813
The first line and stamp 5 pass.

183
00:10:40,833 --> 00:10:45,063
But the last line fails, and so does the
number of lines.

184
00:10:45,083 --> 00:10:49,461
This is an off-by-one error, the same kind
as in Chapter 7.

185
00:10:50,053 --> 00:10:51,600
The fix is on line 2.

186
00:10:51,620 --> 00:10:57,044
The stop value must be one more than the
last stamp: range with 1 and 11.

187
00:10:57,064 --> 00:11:00,102
Now stamp 10 is there, with its free tea.

188
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:02,717
A fix can break something else.

189
00:11:02,737 --> 00:11:05,970
So Asha runs every check again, on the
fixed code.

190
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,967
All four pass, and the output matches her
while version, line by line.

191
00:11:11,587 --> 00:11:12,570
Stage 5: debug.

192
00:11:12,590 --> 00:11:15,512
Now the customer types the size of the
card.

193
00:11:15,532 --> 00:11:17,585
Python stops with a Type Error.

194
00:11:17,605 --> 00:11:22,267
Asha pastes the code and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

195
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:27,408
She reads the error herself too: line 3,
can only concatenate.

196
00:11:27,428 --> 00:11:30,220
She met that message in Chapter 4.

197
00:11:30,267 --> 00:11:31,220
The AI agrees.

198
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:34,418
Input always gives text, so size plus 1
fails.

199
00:11:34,438 --> 00:11:36,226
The fix is int, on line 2.

200
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:40,950
Asha applies the fix on line 2, and runs
it again with 10.

201
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,812
Then she runs all four checks again.

202
00:11:43,832 --> 00:11:47,509
First line, stamp 5, last line, and ten
stamps.

203
00:11:47,529 --> 00:11:48,217
All pass.

204
00:11:48,820 --> 00:11:49,909
Stage 6: improve.

205
00:11:49,929 --> 00:11:55,324
Asha shows both loops, her while loop and
the fixed for loop, and asks which one is

206
00:11:55,344 --> 00:11:55,846
clearer.

207
00:11:55,866 --> 00:11:58,390
She adds: do not change what it prints.

208
00:11:58,433 --> 00:12:02,935
The AI chooses the for loop: there is no
counter line to forget.

209
00:12:02,955 --> 00:12:07,669
Then it suggests one change: range with
10, and print stamp plus 1.

210
00:12:07,689 --> 00:12:10,071
It says the output stays the same.

211
00:12:10,687 --> 00:12:16,780
Asha runs the suggestion, and compares its
output with her card, line by line.

212
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:21,248
Line 2 now counts from 0, so every print
adds 1 to stamp.

213
00:12:32,613 --> 00:12:33,869
Stamps 5 and 10.

214
00:12:33,889 --> 00:12:36,500
Free tea is now in small letters.

215
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:40,009
Asha had said: do not change what it
prints.

216
00:12:40,029 --> 00:12:41,603
The AI ignored that.

217
00:12:41,633 --> 00:12:47,193
And stamp plus 1, written three times, is
harder to read than range with 1 and 11,

218
00:12:47,213 --> 00:12:49,779
which matches the numbers on the card.

219
00:12:49,799 --> 00:12:51,820
Asha keeps her fixed for loop.

220
00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:53,222
Quick check.

221
00:12:53,242 --> 00:12:56,303
An AI says its loop prints the same as
yours.

222
00:12:56,323 --> 00:12:57,947
What do you check first?

223
00:13:09,313 --> 00:13:09,432
A.

224
00:13:09,452 --> 00:13:12,205
Count the lines, and read the last line.

225
00:13:12,225 --> 00:13:14,562
Off-by-one errors hide at the end.

226
00:13:15,153 --> 00:13:15,779
Breather.

227
00:13:15,799 --> 00:13:21,443
Asha caught two AI mistakes: a missing
stamp, and a change she did not ask for.

228
00:13:21,467 --> 00:13:25,526
Next, the four mistakes beginners make
most with for loops.

229
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:31,244
Now you try.

230
00:13:31,264 --> 00:13:34,116
Asha wants to serve tables 1 to 3.

231
00:13:34,136 --> 00:13:35,383
Look at line 2.

232
00:13:46,747 --> 00:13:47,446
Table 3.

233
00:13:47,466 --> 00:13:50,501
Range with 1 and 3 stops before 3.

234
00:13:50,521 --> 00:13:53,107
It is the AI's mistake again.

235
00:13:53,133 --> 00:13:55,453
No error, only a missing table.

236
00:13:55,473 --> 00:14:00,360
The fix: the stop value is one more than
the last value you want.

237
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:01,794
Range with 1 and 4.

238
00:14:02,387 --> 00:14:03,155
Mistake 2.

239
00:14:03,175 --> 00:14:05,991
Range works only with whole numbers.

240
00:14:06,011 --> 00:14:10,877
Zero point five is a float, so Python
stops with a Type Error.

241
00:14:10,900 --> 00:14:12,805
The fix: use whole numbers.

242
00:14:12,825 --> 00:14:17,154
If a number comes from input, convert it
with int, not float.

243
00:14:17,753 --> 00:14:18,383
One more.

244
00:14:18,403 --> 00:14:22,500
This is step 4 again, with one line in a
different place.

245
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:25,172
Compare it with step 4, line by line.

246
00:14:36,547 --> 00:14:39,088
143.25, only the last day.

247
00:14:39,108 --> 00:14:45,887
Line 3 is inside the loop, so the total
goes back to 0 on every pass.

248
00:14:45,933 --> 00:14:48,373
No error, only a wrong total.

249
00:14:48,393 --> 00:14:52,614
The fix: set the total to 0 once, before
the loop.

250
00:14:53,220 --> 00:14:53,911
Mistake 4.

251
00:14:53,931 --> 00:14:57,609
Every for line ends with a colon, like if
and while.

252
00:14:57,629 --> 00:15:00,880
Without it, Python stops before anything
runs.

253
00:15:00,900 --> 00:15:04,241
The error even says what it expected: a
colon.

254
00:15:04,853 --> 00:15:05,733
Last breather.

255
00:15:05,753 --> 00:15:09,073
Now you know the four most common for loop
mistakes.

256
00:15:09,100 --> 00:15:12,388
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

257
00:15:17,117 --> 00:15:17,555
First.

258
00:15:17,575 --> 00:15:21,608
Only even-numbered tables, from 2 to 20,
get a stamp.

259
00:15:21,628 --> 00:15:24,973
Which one range gives exactly those
numbers?

260
00:15:36,347 --> 00:15:39,111
Think about the start, the stop and the
step.

261
00:15:39,131 --> 00:15:41,463
Predict the values first, then run it.

262
00:15:42,053 --> 00:15:42,493
Second.

263
00:15:42,513 --> 00:15:47,939
How could a for loop count every letter a
in the name Anna Abbas, capital or small?

264
00:15:59,313 --> 00:16:02,721
Think about which string method from
Chapter 3 can help.

265
00:16:03,317 --> 00:16:04,357
Let's recap.

266
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:08,320
A for loop runs once for each value in a
range or a string.

267
00:16:08,367 --> 00:16:13,367
Range stops before the stop value, and
range with one number starts at 0.

268
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:15,347
A negative step counts down.

269
00:16:15,367 --> 00:16:18,720
And a running total starts at 0 before the
loop.

270
00:16:18,767 --> 00:16:22,472
Use for when you know the number of
passes, and while when you wait for

271
00:16:22,492 --> 00:16:23,013
something.

272
00:16:23,033 --> 00:16:28,530
And with AI: run its loop and yours with
the same values, and compare every line.

273
00:16:29,153 --> 00:16:30,364
One last check.

274
00:16:30,384 --> 00:16:32,251
Range with 0, 10 and 4.

275
00:16:32,271 --> 00:16:34,466
Which numbers does it give?

276
00:16:45,847 --> 00:16:46,011
B.

277
00:16:46,031 --> 00:16:47,024
0, 4 and 8.

278
00:16:47,044 --> 00:16:51,352
The next value, 12, is past the stop
value, 10.

279
00:16:51,953 --> 00:16:53,248
Mission complete!

280
00:16:53,268 --> 00:16:56,882
You earned 100 XP and the Pattern Runner
badge.

281
00:16:59,117 --> 00:17:00,779
Now it is your turn.

282
00:17:00,799 --> 00:17:04,817
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

283
00:17:04,867 --> 00:17:07,837
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

284
00:17:07,867 --> 00:17:11,787
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including a times table.

285
00:17:11,833 --> 00:17:14,532
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

286
00:17:14,552 --> 00:17:18,278
In the last one, an AI's total is 45
instead of 55.

287
00:17:18,298 --> 00:17:20,923
Find the missing number, and fix it.

288
00:17:20,967 --> 00:17:23,993
List the values of each range before you
run it.

289
00:17:24,013 --> 00:17:26,723
Try each task before you open the
solution.

290
00:17:27,317 --> 00:17:29,822
Today Asha met three kinds of problem.

291
00:17:29,842 --> 00:17:32,280
A syntax error, before anything runs.

292
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:34,739
A type error, while the program runs.

293
00:17:34,759 --> 00:17:37,397
And a wrong total, with no error at all.

294
00:17:37,433 --> 00:17:43,280
In Chapter 9, you will learn to tell them
apart, read a full traceback, and debug

295
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:44,873
with AI, step by step.

296
00:17:44,900 --> 00:17:46,588
Do the warm-up tasks now.

297
00:17:46,608 --> 00:17:48,500
They take about ten minutes.

298
00:17:48,533 --> 00:17:50,200
See you in Chapter 9.
